.TH RADIUSD 8 2026-11-23 "Peios radius 0.1.0"
.SH NAME
radiusd \- the Peios RADIUS service, which authenticates Wi-Fi clients for access points
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B radiusd
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B radiusd
is a RADIUS server for WPA-Enterprise Wi-Fi.
Access points send it each client's EAP conversation, and it decides whether the person may join the network.
It accepts PEAP with MSCHAPv2, and TTLS with PAP, and has
.BR authd (8)
verify each password or MSCHAPv2 response.
It never sees a reusable password hash, and creates no token or logon session.
.PP
A person is admitted by the access policy for the network's SSID: its Connect security descriptor is checked against the verified person's SID, groups and claims, in the kernel, and an assignment rule may give their session settings such as a VLAN.
An Access-Accept asks the access point to reauthenticate the client after an hour by default.
.B radiusd
can also take accounting records, and send Disconnect and Change-of-Authorization requests for the sessions they describe.
.PP
peinit starts
.B radiusd
as the
.B radiusd
service, under its own virtual service account; you do not run it yourself.
It refuses to start under any other identity, and takes no arguments.
Manage it, and its configuration, with
.BR radius (8).
.SH CONFIGURATION
.B radiusd
reads its configuration from the Registry under
.BR Machine\eSoftware\eRadius ,
which
.B radius config init
creates, and takes up each change as it is written, without restarting.
While the configuration is invalid it admits nobody, and says why through its status endpoint and its events.
Its clients' shared secrets, and its server identities' certificates and private keys, are files the configuration names, normally under
.IR /var/state/radius/credentials .
Run
.B regman Machine\eSoftware\eRadius
for each value.
.PP
It listens for authentication on UDP port 1812 of every IPv4 address by default.
The radius package reserves UDP ports 1812 and 1813 for it.
.SH SECURITY
The service account may ask
.B authd
to verify credentials (the
.B peios.auth.authenticate
capability), read each person's groups and claims, read its configuration and credential files, and bind its ports.
It holds SeChangeNotifyPrivilege and SeAuditPrivilege, and nothing else.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The service stopped.
.TP
.B 1
.B radiusd
failed, for example because it was given an argument, runs under the wrong identity, or could not open a local endpoint or start a worker.
An authentication listener bind failure leaves the service running with invalid configuration; inspect
.B radius status
for the error.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /run/radiusd/control.sock
The read-only status endpoint
.B radius status
reads.
.TP
.I /run/radiusd/accounting.sock
The endpoint
.B radius
reads accounting sessions from and sends session controls through.
.TP
.I /var/state/radius/credentials
Shared secrets, certificates and private keys.
.TP
.I /var/state/radius/accounting
The accounting ledger.
.SH NOTES
The radius package defines the service, grants its identity what it needs and reserves its ports in the Registry seeds
.IR radiusd\-service.reg ,
.I radiusd\-policy.reg
and
.IR radiusd\-port.reg .
Installing the package does not start the service.
After configuring it, run
.B radius service start
to start it now, and
.B radius service boot on
to enable startup on subsequent boots.
.PP
Creating
.I /var/state/radius
with its descriptor needs a kernel with KACS control handles; on an older one,
.B radius config init
fails rather than create the directory unprotected.
.SH READINESS
.B radiusd
tells peinit it is ready once its status endpoint is listening, even while its configuration is invalid, so that
.B radius
can say what is wrong.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR evman (1),
.BR regman (1),
.BR authd (8),
.BR radius (8)
.PP
